# Agentic Cross-Test Review

## Instructions

The goal of this review is to evaluate every test in `## Test Files` against the other listed tests.
Review the files one at a time. For each test in each file, evaluate every criterion under `## Review Criteria`.
Create one `## Review Scorecard` for each test, and update every row for the test under review.
Set each result to exactly one of `pass` or `fail`.
For every failure, provide concrete notes naming the relevant tests.

Evaluate each criterion in a fresh subagent.
Do not inspect manifests, non-test repository files, prior conversation, or unlisted tests.
If a listed file is unavailable, fail with notes identifying the missing file.

## Review Criteria

### 1x. Requirement Distinctiveness

#### 11. Requirements Are Distinct
Extract `Requirement Tested` from every test docstring and compare the requirements.
Each requirement shall describe behavior and a scenario that distinguish it from requirements at the same test level.
Fail requirements that overlap because their wording is too generic, and recommend more specific wording.
Do not fail intentional cross-level overlap under this criterion; evaluate that overlap under criteria 21 through 23.

### 2x. Cross-Level Relationships

#### 21. Cross-Level Pairs Are Identified
Identify every test pair that verifies the same or a substantially similar requirement at different levels.
Examples include a private-function test paired with a public-function test, or a unit test paired with an integration test.
If no cross-level pair exists, pass and state that no pair was found.

#### 22. Cross-Level Pairs Reference Each Other
Every test in a cross-level pair shall reference the other test in `Verification Detail`.
In `Verification Detail`, name both tests and state the level covered by each test.
Use wording equivalent to: `This is a <X>-level test; see also <test_name>, which tests the same requirement at the <Y> level.`
Fail if either test omits the reciprocal reference or does not state both test levels.
If no cross-level pair exists, pass as not applicable.

#### 23. Lower-Level Tests Are Justified
The lower-level test in every cross-level pair shall justify why it is required in `Verification Detail`.
The justification shall use one of these reasons:

- A. Shared foundation: The lower-level service supports multiple high-level use cases and therefore requires direct testing.
- B. Deeper coverage: The high-level tests cover only part of the requirement, while the lower-level test covers it more deeply.
- C. Diagnostic completeness: The lower-level test is not required for behavior coverage but is retained for completeness or clearer fault diagnosis.

Fail a lower-level test that omits the justification or does not explain how the selected reason applies.
If no cross-level pair exists, pass as not applicable.

